      "dom_id": "question-49067",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "What is the antiderivative of ${(1+\\frac{1}{x})}^{2}?$",
      "worked_solution": "Expanding the binomial and applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1+\\frac{1}{x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int (1+\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{{x}^{-2+1}}{-2+1}+C \\\\  & =x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\frac{1}{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1+\\frac{1}{x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =x-\\frac{1}{x}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =x-\\frac{1}{x}+2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡K) \\\\  & =x-\\frac{1}{x}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
